=[ catasto ]

Generated by `catasto spec cli` from the clap definitions in `app/src/cli.rs`. Do not edit it: change the doc comments there and run the command again.

catasto keeps the files a workspace takes from elsewhere -- a vocabulary, a shared schema -- where its manifest says, each pinned to a commit and a SHA-256.

Start with `catasto init`, name a place with `catasto upstream add`, take a file from it with `catasto add`, and let `catasto check` say whether anything has drifted.

```text
catasto [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
```

Options:

- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto init ]

Create catasto.ctmt, a manifest that declares nothing yet

```text
catasto init [OPTIONS]
```

Options:

- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto add ]

Take one file from a place: declare it, fetch it and lock it.

The other files stay where they are. If the fetch fails the manifest is put back, so a failed `add` leaves nothing behind.

```text
catasto add [OPTIONS] <--upstream <NAME>|--git <URL>|--local> <DEST> [FROM]
```

Arguments:

- `<DEST>`: Where the file goes, relative to the workspace root.
- `[FROM]`: Where the file is in the place it comes from: a path inside the upstream, inside the repository, or on disk with --local. Defaults to DEST (except with --local, which requires the path).

Options:

- `--upstream <NAME>`: Take it from a place named in `::upstreams`.
- `--git <URL>`: Take it from this git repository (`https://` or a local path).
- `--local`: Take it from a file on disk, relative to the workspace root.
- `--rev <REV>`: The tag, branch or commit to read, with --git. Default: `main`.
- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto remove ]

Stop managing a file: undeclare it and forget what the lock says.

The file stays on disk unless --delete is given.

```text
catasto remove [OPTIONS] <DEST>
```

Arguments:

- `<DEST>`: The file, as the manifest writes its path.

Options:

- `--delete`: Delete the file too.
- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto upstream ]

Name the places files are taken from

```text
catasto upstream [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
```

Options:

- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

===[ catasto upstream add ]

Name a git repository, so entries can say `upstream: NAME`

```text
catasto upstream add [OPTIONS] <NAME> <GIT>
```

Arguments:

- `<NAME>`: The name, letters, digits, `_` and `-`.
- `<GIT>`: An `https://` URL, or a local path relative to the workspace root.

Options:

- `--rev <REV>`: The tag, branch or commit to read. Default: `main`.
- `--root <ROOT>`: The directory of the repository that the entries' paths start from.
- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

===[ catasto upstream remove ]

Drop a name; refused while a file still uses it

```text
catasto upstream remove [OPTIONS] <NAME>
```

Arguments:

- `<NAME>`: The name.

Options:

- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

===[ catasto upstream list ]

List the names and what they point at

```text
catasto upstream list [OPTIONS]
```

Options:

- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto list ]

Show every declared file: where it comes from, and how it stands

```text
catasto list [OPTIONS]
```

Options:

- `--json`: Print JSON, for tools.
- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto check ]

Check the directory tree against its schema and lockfile.

Exits 1 when a file has drifted, and says which command puts it right. It never touches the network.

```text
catasto check [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Workspace root. Defaults to the current directory.

Options:

- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto lock ]

Record the sha256 of every managed file in catasto.lock

```text
catasto lock [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Workspace root. Defaults to the current directory.

Options:

- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto sync ]

Re-fetch managed files from their sources and rewrite catasto.lock.

Git sources are read at the commit recorded in catasto.lock.

```text
catasto sync [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Workspace root. Defaults to the current directory.

Options:

- `--update`: Resolve every git rev again and lock the commits they point at now.
- `--only <FILE>`: Only this file (as the manifest writes its path); repeat for more. The others, and what the lock says about them, stay as they are.
- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).

==[ catasto spec ]

Generate automated .tmt specifications and reference documentation

```text
catasto spec [OPTIONS] [TARGET]
```

Arguments:

- `[TARGET]`: Documentation target to generate (`cli` | `manifest`).

Options:

- `--all`: Generate all declared specification targets. Requires `--out <DIR>`. Conflicts with `[TARGET]`.
- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this file or directory instead of stdout.
- `--check`: Fail if output files are missing or out of date, without writing.
- `-C, --workspace <DIR>`: The workspace root. Defaults to the current directory. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take it as a trailing path, like the other tools of the ecosystem. (`upstream add --root` is something else: a directory inside the repository it names.).
